[b]Men tire. Lines hold. A fresh charge at the right moment breaks them.[/b]

Slower battles, where timing, rest and defence decide the fight — and heroes matter.

[url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jYq9nJX9dok]Watch a battle with the mod on YouTube[/url]

[h1]The idea[/h1]
[list]
[*][b]Men tire.[/b] Every swing, kick and block costs stamina. A spent soldier attacks once where a fresh one attacks five times.
[*][b]Tired men are careful, not brave.[/b] They hold their place, block and wait for an opening — nobody trades blows until he drops.
[*][b]An order makes them surge.[/b] Call the charge and the line surges in with a fever that burns out as fast as their strength.
[*][b]A shield wall holds.[/b] Men keep their places; a man outnumbered pulls back to his mates instead of dying alone.
[*][b]A fresh unit hitting a tired line throws men around[/b] — staggered, knocked down, shields and riders knocked away.
[*][b]Skill and timing decide the end of a long fight.[/b] When both men are spent, every wrong block and every swing into a good guard counts.
[*][b]Heroes matter.[/b] They tire slowest; late in a battle they are often the freshest men on the field.
[/list]

[h1]New in v1.3[/h1]
[list]
[*][b]Each man rests on his own.[/b] A tired man stops pushing, steps back out of reach still facing the enemy, and goes back in when he has his breath — or when the man beside him goes. When both lines are spent, a gap opens by itself.
[*][b]Fresh men rotate forward.[/b] A tired front man swaps places with a fresher mate behind him.
[*][b]Packs, not swarms.[/b] Men press against the enemy line instead of drifting through it or wrapping round its ends. Only an order sends them round a flank.
[*][b]A shield wall stands firm[/b] on a hold order — its men strike from their places — and pushes forward as one.
[*][b]The clinch.[/b] Men kick and shield-bash as part of their fighting. Meet a bash with a guard to the right, or a kick with a low guard, and the attacker is thrown off balance; meet it wrong and you are.
[*][b]Spearmen hold with the spear,[/b] and a thrust passes the friend in front of him.
[*][b]War cries.[/b] A charge order makes the line roar; a man going back in shouts.
[*][b]Discipline by troop tier.[/b] Green troops will not stretch into a thin line; frightened men flock together behind their mates.
[*][b]No accidental charges[/b] — dragging an order over an enemy banner no longer sends your men after it.
[/list]

[h1]How a battle plays[/h1]
[b]Tiredness and rest.[/b] Every fighter carries an Athletics bar — his stamina, as big as his Athletics skill. Fresh, he fights as in the base game. Spent, he waits between attacks, runs at a jog, his lucky hits are gone, and the AI starts guessing which way to block. Anyone can save a few swings and strike them back to back. A minute of rest refills him — and a skilful defender recovers [i]while[/i] he defends: clean blocks and parries let the bar climb, a block on the wrong side stops it.

[b]Your orders and the fever of a charge.[/b] Holding men stop swinging early and save their strength behind their shields, advancing men give more, charging men give everything. Order the charge and they surge — the line goes in eager, sprinting the last metres — and the surge fades as the fever burns. A rested line hits hard and long; a tired one surges briefly. The charge is a moment to choose, not a standing order.

[b]Holding the line.[/b] A shield wall stays a wall: each man keeps his place and fights what reaches him, with one short step out to strike. Men weigh the odds around them. The looter who wanders round your flank alone looks at your wall, thinks better of it, and walks back to his mates; a crowd wraps round your wall only when it truly outnumbers you there. Shaken men pack together; hopeless ones panic and run. Tired cavalry stops trickling in one by one: riders gather with their own kind and come back as waves.

[b]Blows that count.[/b] On a fresh man nothing changes. On a tired one a kick can knock his shield away or put him on the ground, a hit can stagger him, a rider can be knocked from his saddle — and a man still reeling is easy prey. A correct block punishes the attacker: swing tired into a good guard and [i]you[/i] reel. A man running into his blow hits harder, so hold a fresh shock unit back, wait until the enemy line has fought for two minutes, and charge it into their flank at a run: men go down, shields fly, the line folds.

[b]Duels and tournaments.[/b] A long bout is won by stamina and timing. The champion who never misses a block starts guessing when he is spent; your kick that bounces off a fresh king floors an empty one. Tire him first, then break him.

[b]What you see.[/b] Your Athletics bar under your health, your stored swings and your sprint above it. Hold ALT and every formation — yours and the enemy's — shows its men's Athletics, health and how many are still [i]ready[/i] to fight; "ready 12/48" in red under an enemy marker is an invitation. Hold Left Ctrl to sprint — a short dodge, paid from your stamina.

Read more: [url=https://github.com/TraxData313/trax_combat_enhancements/blob/main/docs/HOW-IT-WORKS.md]the player guide[/url] (every system in plain words, with tactics) and [url=https://github.com/TraxData313/trax_combat_enhancements/blob/main/docs/MECHANICS.md]the mechanics reference[/url] (every table and number).

[h1]Settings[/h1]
It works out of the box. With [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=2859238197]MCM[/url] ([b]optional[/b]) you get a short page — the master switch, how hard hits land, how fast men tire, each AI system's own switch, staggers, the sprint, what the screen shows — and an [b]Advanced[/b] page with every other number. Without MCM the same settings sit in a commented config file. Every change applies at once, even mid-battle; the master switch turns the whole mod off mid-fight, so you can play the same battle both ways.

[h1]Good to know[/h1]
[list]
[*][b]Not an overhaul.[/b] Vanilla troops, weapons and AI — it changes how a fight feels, not what is in it. Nothing in your save: add or remove it mid-campaign.
[*]Game v1.4.8, singleplayer. No requirements, no Harmony. Works beside RTS Camera and War Sails.
[*][b]Not with Realistic Battle Mod (RBM)[/b] — the deep, much-loved overhaul with its own stamina. This is the light road on vanilla's troops; use one or the other.
[/list]

Bugs, balance, a mod it fights with: tell me in the comments or on [url=https://github.com/TraxData313/trax_combat_enhancements/issues]GitHub[/url]. Attaching [i]trax_combat.log[/i] (beside the config file) helps a lot.

Made by two of us: the code by Claude (Opus 5.5), an AI; the idea and playtesting by [url=https://github.com/TraxData313]Trax[/url], Bible believer and engineer in AI/ML/Python/Applied Maths.

